WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.369
- The United States right now.

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.137
I don't believe I haven't
looked at those figures

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:05.638
in a while,

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.841
but I don't think that the
United States is the largest

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.075
polluter in the world.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that China and
India are above us.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.714
Now, here you have four
and a half billion people

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:17.083
who are trying to get rich quick

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.719
and who are spewing a lot
of CO2 into the atmosphere

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:21.755
and who are also very
competitive with us

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.723
because they have
very low wages.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:28.495
So, to tie ourselves to
the Paris Climate Accord

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.530
was the wrong way
to go about it.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.432 align:start size:81% position:19%
And you know, what
else happened?

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.834
Was that the United
States has decreased

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.771
its emissions of CO2 per capita

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:41.508
at a faster pace than most
places in the world. Why?

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:46.479
Because we started getting
rid of our coal power plants

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:49.482
and we started introducing
more and more natural gas

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:53.720 align:start position:0%
into our electricity production
and natural gas burns,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.623
or rather emits much
less CO2 than coal.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:01:00.193
And so by simply, by innovating,

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:02.362
by basically changing
the source of fuel

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:03.930
from coal to natural gas,

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.433 align:start size:88% position:13%
we were able to decrease
our CO2 emissions

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:09.469
at a faster click than we
would have been obliged

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:11.371
to do under Paris.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.940
(upbeat music)

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.913 line:0%
- I am Dave Rubin. And
this is The Rubin Report.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:23.383 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Joining me today is the
Editor of HUMANPROGRESS.ORG

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.518
and the co-author
of the new book,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:29.189
"10 Global Trends, Every
Smart Person Should Know"

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.858
and many others that you
will find interesting.

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
Marian Tupy welcome back
to The Rubin Report.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:36.629
- Thank you very
much for having me.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:37.797
- All right, Marian.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:41.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
It is a week before
the election.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.203
Everyone is going
completely insane.

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.404
And I thought, all right,

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:46.906 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're gonna talk
about some good news.

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.275
And that is exactly what
this book is all about.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.777
And I absolutely loved it

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:54.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you've got graphs
and you've got charts

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:57.917
and you've got pictures
and it's a pleasant way

00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:59.486
of getting some information,

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:02.288
but I thought the best way
to kick this off with you

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:06.826
would be why do you
think people are so

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:09.929
hesitant to believe that
good things are happening?

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.966
When I quote this book just
in private conversations

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:14.868
or when I have talks with people

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:16.302 align:start size:81% position:19%
that things aren't
getting so bad,

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.037
there's a real resistance to it.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:19.606
Like they can't believe it.

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.608
Have you guys thought
about that at all?

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.244 line:0%
- Yes, absolutely.

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:27.981 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Look, human beings have
evolved to be pessimistic,

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:32.685 line:0%
essentially our species is
about 300,000 years old.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:36.823
And for most of that time,
life was extremely difficult.

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:40.693 align:start size:97% position:3%
The world was much less
hospitable to human beings.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.663
And so we paid to be pessimistic

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:46.232
to expect bad things happen.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.967
Any organism

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.235
that

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.671
understates the threats

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:56.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
may then be destroyed,
eaten, killed by predators.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.281 align:start position:0%
But if you overstate the threat
nothing bad happens to you,

00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:05.218
except you may lose a bit
of time or a bit of energy.

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.854
You know, if you're
going around a bush

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.523
and you hear some noise there

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.725 align:start position:0%
and you think, well,
everything is going to be fine,

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:16.529 align:start size:97% position:3%
but in fact, there is a tiger
or lion behind the bush.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:20.600
Then that under reaction is
going to cost you your life.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.470 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you're not going to
become an ancestor to anybody.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.074
Whereas if you hear a
noise and you think,

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:30.610
well, there may be a lion
or a tiger behind that bush

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.078
and you run away.

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:34.280
Then of course, you're
going to pass those genes

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.917
onto your progeny
and over millennia,

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:41.120
hundreds of thousands
of years, those genes,

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.891
which are pessimistic are
probably going to survive

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:47.860
and they're going to
promote themselves.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.763 align:start size:97% position:3%
Whereas the more
pessimistic ones presumably,

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.232
would be weeded out
of the gene pool.

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.700
So that's part of the reason,

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's the hard
wiring of the human brain,

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:58.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you also have the soft
wiring of human brain.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:01.808
Things like things
like, for example,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:07.313
people fear losses more than
they look forward to gains.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.949
This is called a
negativity bias,

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:12.719 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is called bad
is stronger than good.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
We always remember the things,
money that we have lost,

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
but we forget the
money that we have won.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:24.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
We remember the
criticism from the boss,

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.966
but we don't remember all
the good things they said

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.301
about our work.

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:30.470
And there are a lot of
these negativity biases,

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.006
which have developed over
millennia in our brains

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:35.742
and they make us into a
very pessimistic species.

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:37.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. It's also
interesting because you know,

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:38.811
people always complain,

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:40.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
Oh, well, if you
watch the local news,

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:43.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's so depressing because
it's just you know,

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:45.551
car accident, guy got shot.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.588
You know, baby fell off
the roof blah-blah-blah

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:50.590
and yet people watch.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.126
And if they just showed
good news in essence,

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:55.295
people just wouldn't watch.

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:58.631 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That's right. So we have
evolved to look for bad things

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.366 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be on the lookout
for bad things.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.168
That's what grabs our attention.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.973
There was a psychological
experiment a few years ago

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:11.878
where the psychologist
asked the cohort of,

00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:14.781 align:start size:97% position:3%
well, the cohort of people,
whom they were testing,

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.249
the testing cohort.

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:18.718 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are you interested more in
the bad news or the good news?

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:21.220
And the cohorts said we are
interested in the good news.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.689
Absolutely. For sure.

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:25.491
And then on a split screen,

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:28.127
this psychologist started
showing people bad news

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.629
and good news.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:33.032
And immediately people's eyes
tended towards the bad news.

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:36.202 align:start size:97% position:3%
We are always on the
lookout for the bad things.

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.970
If it bleeds, it leads.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.673
That's why the media
leads with bad news

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:45.044
because that's what
we are programmed to
consume with vigor.

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:46.412
- All right. So with that,

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.115
as the backdrop for
our conversation,

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:52.852
one week before the big
presidential election,

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
I want to go into the top
10 that you talk about here,

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:59.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the book has 78 trends
that are going well.

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:00.927
So, we're gonna plow
through the top 10,

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:03.930
and then I'm just gonna quiz
you on a couple of random ones

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.565
as well, but I'm going to
go through them right here.

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:07.600
So, the first trend
that you guys have

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.135
is the great enrichment.

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:12.105
And this is basically
about the economy.

00:06:12.105 --> 00:06:13.272
So, can you lay this out?

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:15.475 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because a lot of people
think we're poor.

00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:16.943
The divide is worse than ever,

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:20.346 align:start position:0%
and we seem to be getting poor,
but it's actually not true.

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:24.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Since the historical
record begun,

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:29.756
people lived on about
$2 per person per day,

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:31.691
2000 years ago at
the time of Christ

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:36.362
or Caesar Augustus, people in
America, in Europe, in Africa,

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:39.866
anywhere in the world,
lived on $2 a day or so.

00:06:39.866 --> 00:06:43.403
That is why today when we
talk about absolute poverty

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:45.738
about living at a
subsistence level,

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.641
we talk about that $2 level

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:51.277
because that's how
people used to live

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:52.879
since the dawn of time really.

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:57.450
Now bring it back to
or bring it forward

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.718
all the way to 1800s

00:06:58.718 --> 00:07:01.587
at the time of Thomas
Jefferson presidency,

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:06.559
global income per capita
per day is only $2.80.

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:08.428
So it's still very, very low.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:10.863
But today 200 years later,

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:14.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
it is $44 per person
per day globally

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:19.172 align:start position:0%
in the United States is roughly
$160 per person per day.

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:20.006
And

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:22.742
if the current trends continue,

00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:25.478
if we are able to repeat
the growth that we had

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:30.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
over the last hundred years
and project it to 2100,

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:35.855 align:start position:0%
then average global income will
be $144 per person per day.

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:40.393
So from $2, since
the dawn of time,

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:42.762
to $44 today,

00:07:42.762 --> 00:07:45.531
to about 150 in 2100,

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:48.601
that's the extent of the
tremendous economic growth

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.837
that we have experienced
in the last 200 years.

00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:52.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that we can still
experience in the future.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:55.208 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I think, one of the
things that confuses people,

00:07:55.208 --> 00:07:57.443
at least when we hear this
through the mainstream media,

00:07:57.443 --> 00:08:00.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
is there's this idea that
the rich keep getting richer

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:03.316
and that the poor
are getting poorer,

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:05.485 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the argument that you're
laying out in essence,

00:08:05.485 --> 00:08:07.220
it's not that the rich
aren't getting richer.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.788
Many rich people
are getting richer,

00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:12.592
but that the entire pool
is actually being raised

00:08:12.592 --> 00:08:14.126
at the same time.

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:17.497
So that the lowest person
is in far better shape now

00:08:17.497 --> 00:08:19.599 align:start size:81% position:19%
than they were
say 200 years ago.

00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:22.068
- That's correct.

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:24.770
The data which I have just
presented are data based

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.740
on global average, which
is to say a person living

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
maybe somewhere in
Indonesia or Malaysia,

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.444
right in the middle of the pack.

00:08:32.545 --> 00:08:34.447
The data is not distorted by

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:39.185
you know looking at
just very rich people

00:08:39.185 --> 00:08:41.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
and high income inequality
or what have you.

00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:44.790 align:start size:97% position:3%
Globally, we are much better
off than we used to be,

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:48.327
of course, in well
advanced countries,

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.196 align:start size:84% position:16%
rich countries, people
are much richer,

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:52.265 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the world as a
whole is getting richer,

00:08:52.265 --> 00:08:54.734 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is why poverty
has declined.

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:56.502
Absolute poverty has declined

00:08:56.502 --> 00:08:59.105
for about 90% of
humanity in 1800

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:01.073
to less than 8% today.

00:09:01.073 --> 00:09:03.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
I just want to
repeat that in 1800,

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.510
just 200 years ago,

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:09.515
90% of people lived on
$2 per person per day.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:14.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
Today, roughly 8% of people
live on $2 per person per day.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.054
So,

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:19.825 align:start size:81% position:19%
yes, the great
increase in riches

00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:23.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
has resulted in a
substantial reduction

00:09:23.796 --> 00:09:25.364
in absolute poverty.

00:09:25.364 --> 00:09:28.601
We have seen a billion people
rise from absolute poverty

00:09:28.601 --> 00:09:30.970 align:start size:84% position:16%
in China in the
last 40 or 50 years.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.072
We are seeing India coming up.

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.341
We are seeing a number of
African countries growing

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:36.742
at 5% per year.

00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:40.513 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a lot of good news
that's happening in the world,

00:09:40.513 --> 00:09:42.615
or at least there
was before COVID.

00:09:42.615 --> 00:09:45.384 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And since this
election is a week away,

00:09:45.384 --> 00:09:47.753
we should talk about the forces

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:49.288
that make all of that happen.

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:52.258
It has a little something to
do with free markets, right?

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:54.894
- That's correct.

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:58.364 align:start size:97% position:3%
The greatest decline in
absolute poverty in the world

00:09:58.364 --> 00:10:00.766 line:0%
is a core terminus

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:04.770 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
with the opening of the
global economy after 1980.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.073 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
This is called the
period of globalization.

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:09.108
Now I realized that
a lot of people,

00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:10.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
including a lot of people
in the United States

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:13.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
are ambivalent
about globalization,

00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:17.116
but there is no doubt that
once these formerly poor

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:20.553 align:start size:97% position:3%
countries dropped socialism
and central planning

00:10:20.553 --> 00:10:24.457 align:start position:0%
embraced free markets, embraced
multinational corporations,

00:10:24.457 --> 00:10:26.559
and inward foreign investment.

00:10:26.559 --> 00:10:29.662
Once that happened, these
countries started to grow

00:10:29.662 --> 00:10:32.565 align:start size:84% position:16%
at a much faster pace,
which is why today

00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:35.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
places like China are
middle income countries.

00:10:35.735 --> 00:10:37.370
They are no longer dirt poor.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.305
I mean, at the time of Mao,

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:41.707
these people literally ate dirt.

00:10:41.707 --> 00:10:43.876 align:start size:84% position:16%
Today, they're sending
people to the moon,

00:10:43.876 --> 00:10:45.411
well, at least to space.

00:10:45.411 --> 00:10:46.479
So, like all this

00:10:46.479 --> 00:10:49.215
- Yeah working on it

00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:52.551
- The last 40 years have
been exceptionally good,

00:10:52.551 --> 00:10:54.920
especially for poor countries.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.224
- Yeah. All right. Let's
go to number two here,

00:10:58.224 --> 00:10:59.692
which is really an
offshoot of this,

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:03.763
that in many ways, we're
at the end of poverty.

00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:06.532
- Absolute poverty. Yes.

00:11:06.532 --> 00:11:08.634
As you well know in
the United States,

00:11:08.634 --> 00:11:10.503
we no longer have
absolute poverty.

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:12.872
Nobody lives on $2 per day.

00:11:12.872 --> 00:11:16.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
Even people who don't
have a job can access food

00:11:16.509 --> 00:11:18.210
and all sorts of facilities,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.713
which are provided
by the taxpayer.

00:11:20.713 --> 00:11:22.615
But globally,

00:11:22.615 --> 00:11:24.950
absolute poverty had been,

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:28.354
or was before COVID at its
lowest it has ever been,

00:11:28.354 --> 00:11:30.923
which is to say less than 8%.

00:11:30.923 --> 00:11:33.726 align:start size:88% position:13%
And now of course as
countries become richer,

00:11:33.726 --> 00:11:36.662 align:start size:97% position:3%
they start to redefine
poverty in a different way,

00:11:36.662 --> 00:11:38.431 align:start size:81% position:19%
which is why in
the United States.

00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:40.332
We don't talk about
absolute poverty

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:42.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
when we talk about poverty
it's relative poverty,

00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:45.838
it's a certain percentage
of the mean national income

00:11:45.838 --> 00:11:47.606
that we consider if people live

00:11:47.606 --> 00:11:49.108
under that they are in poverty,

00:11:49.108 --> 00:11:51.377
but we don't have absolute
poverty in the United States.

00:11:51.377 --> 00:11:53.713 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So, you know,
from someone sitting

00:11:53.713 --> 00:11:55.981
where I am in Los Angeles,

00:11:55.981 --> 00:12:00.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm seeing many more homeless
people than there were

00:12:00.086 --> 00:12:01.620
a couple of years ago.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.089
We know this is happening
in a lot of the big cities

00:12:03.089 --> 00:12:06.125
in Portland, in Seattle,
certainly in San Francisco.

00:12:06.125 --> 00:12:09.395
I think people see that, you
know, you see tent cities,

00:12:09.395 --> 00:12:12.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
you see more homeless
people, et cetera, et cetera.

00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:14.767
And the inclination is, "Oh,
well, I'm seeing something.

00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:19.505 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it must be significantly
worse than it was."

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:24.343
But is it just that that's
happening in just some cities

00:12:24.343 --> 00:12:26.912 align:start size:88% position:13%
or is it just that
your anecdotal evidence

00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:28.414
doesn't really prove anything?

00:12:29.849 --> 00:12:34.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, whether you look at
poverty as a relative measure,

00:12:34.019 --> 00:12:36.355 align:start size:97% position:3%
relative poverty as a
percentage of national income,

00:12:36.355 --> 00:12:38.124
or what do you look at
consumption poverty.

00:12:38.124 --> 00:12:40.259
In other words, how much
do people actually spend

00:12:40.259 --> 00:12:43.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
on a monthly basis,
a weekly basis?

00:12:43.129 --> 00:12:44.830
Poverty is an all-time low.

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:47.967
I suspect that what's happening
in places like San Francisco

00:12:47.967 --> 00:12:51.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
and Los Angeles is like new
policies have been implemented

00:12:51.804 --> 00:12:55.141
by local governments, which
make it more attractive,

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:02.615
for people who may not
be necessarily interested

00:13:03.182 --> 00:13:04.550
in jobs.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:07.353 align:start position:0%
May not necessarily be
interested in normal lifestyles.

00:13:07.353 --> 00:13:09.755 align:start size:84% position:16%
People who may, for
whatever reason prefer

00:13:12.858 --> 00:13:15.461 align:start size:88% position:13%
the freedom essentially
of not being tied

00:13:15.461 --> 00:13:20.065
to anything concrete and want
to live in these tent cities

00:13:20.065 --> 00:13:22.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
and who may have been
living in those tent cities

00:13:22.535 --> 00:13:25.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
throughout the United States.
But for whatever reason,

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:28.307
because the policies of local
governments have now changed,

00:13:28.307 --> 00:13:29.742
these people are being pulled

00:13:29.742 --> 00:13:32.311
like a magnet towards
certain jurisdictions.

00:13:32.311 --> 00:13:33.779 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's why you
see many more of them.

00:13:33.779 --> 00:13:38.217 align:start position:0%
That would be, I think, one
reason you see that anecdotally?

00:13:38.217 --> 00:13:39.852
- Yeah. And we do
know by the way

00:13:39.852 --> 00:13:42.621
that they're putting
in policies in places

00:13:42.621 --> 00:13:44.123 align:start size:81% position:19%
like San Francisco
and Los Angeles

00:13:44.123 --> 00:13:46.125
that are attracting
more homeless people.

00:13:46.125 --> 00:13:47.793
It's not something that
you're just making up,

00:13:47.793 --> 00:13:49.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
at one point,
there was a policy.

00:13:49.195 --> 00:13:51.130
I'm not sure if we still
have it in Los Angeles,

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:54.366 align:start size:94% position:6%
where if you build a
structure on public land,

00:13:54.366 --> 00:13:56.202
they can't remove it.

00:13:56.202 --> 00:13:59.004
You got an awful lot of
people paying mortgages

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:00.206
on land that they bought.

00:14:00.206 --> 00:14:02.274 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know what
we're doing over here.

00:14:02.274 --> 00:14:03.809
- I would simply add,

00:14:03.809 --> 00:14:07.112
just to clarify one comment I
made in my last contribution.

00:14:07.112 --> 00:14:10.449
And that is that when I say
that people don't necessarily

00:14:10.449 --> 00:14:15.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
want to have a home and work
for a living, et cetera.

00:14:15.187 --> 00:14:16.655
I'm not making a value judgment.

00:14:16.655 --> 00:14:18.791
I'm saying that there
are a lot of people

00:14:18.791 --> 00:14:20.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
who have very
different conceptions

00:14:20.292 --> 00:14:21.861
about what a good life is.

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:23.329
And also in addition to that,

00:14:23.329 --> 00:14:28.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are a lot of people
who may be mentally unstable,

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:30.769
who have all sorts
of mental problems

00:14:30.769 --> 00:14:33.038
that prevents them
from keeping a job

00:14:33.038 --> 00:14:36.208
and remaining in one
place at the same time.

00:14:36.208 --> 00:14:38.177 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right? So, and
naturally you're saying,

00:14:38.177 --> 00:14:41.013 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are things
potentially that government

00:14:41.013 --> 00:14:44.216
or private citizens can
do for those people,

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:48.954
but that overall this is
working and getting better

00:14:48.954 --> 00:14:51.523
- Well, there are two
sides of that ledger.

00:14:51.523 --> 00:14:54.894
On the one hand, you want
to do something for people

00:14:54.894 --> 00:14:56.562
who are the very
bottom of society.

00:14:56.562 --> 00:14:59.531
On the other hand,
that shouldn't mean
that you make life

00:14:59.531 --> 00:15:02.668 align:start size:88% position:13%
unlivable for people
who live in these cities

00:15:02.668 --> 00:15:04.670
and who actually pay taxes.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.138
- Exactly.

00:15:06.138 --> 00:15:08.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
All right. Trend number
three, this is interesting.

00:15:08.374 --> 00:15:10.142
Are we running out of resources?

00:15:10.142 --> 00:15:12.211 align:start size:97% position:3%
Everyone seems to think
we're running out of water.

00:15:12.211 --> 00:15:15.581 align:start position:0%
We're running out of air. We're
running out of green space.

00:15:15.581 --> 00:15:18.284 align:start size:88% position:13%
Are we actually running
out of resources?

00:15:18.284 --> 00:15:20.452 align:start size:88% position:13%
- No, we are not
contrary to what people

00:15:20.452 --> 00:15:23.923
have been worried about
since really the 1960s.

00:15:23.923 --> 00:15:25.457
We are not running
out of resources.

00:15:25.457 --> 00:15:27.459
In fact, resources
are becoming cheaper.

00:15:28.627 --> 00:15:31.664 align:start size:88% position:13%
At HumanProgress, I have
an autonomous section

00:15:31.664 --> 00:15:33.332 align:start size:88% position:13%
of HumanProgress called
the Simon project.

00:15:33.332 --> 00:15:36.235
And in the Simon project,
we have actually calculated

00:15:36.235 --> 00:15:39.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
that for every 1% increase
in human population,

00:15:39.705 --> 00:15:43.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
the price of natural
resources declined by 1%.

00:15:43.309 --> 00:15:47.613
What's happening here
is that more people

00:15:47.613 --> 00:15:50.115
also produce more ideas,

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:53.285 align:start size:81% position:19%
and more ideas
can be put towards

00:15:53.285 --> 00:15:55.387
not just the exploring

00:15:55.387 --> 00:15:57.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
and exploiting greater
part of the earth.

00:15:57.923 --> 00:16:00.726
More ideas can be used

00:16:03.429 --> 00:16:07.166
in order to make us
more efficient in
the use of resources.

00:16:07.166 --> 00:16:08.434
You know, 50, 60 years ago,

00:16:08.434 --> 00:16:10.803
the can of Coke was
much, much heavier

00:16:10.803 --> 00:16:14.807
than what it is today, because
we used so much more aluminum

00:16:14.807 --> 00:16:15.941
in order to produce it.

00:16:17.076 --> 00:16:19.545
But then smart people realized,

00:16:19.545 --> 00:16:23.816
"Hey, why should we spend
so much money on aluminum?"

00:16:23.816 --> 00:16:26.685 align:start position:0%
Because every time you
increase the price of an input,

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:28.487
you increase the
price of an output.

00:16:28.487 --> 00:16:30.255
Why don't we save that money

00:16:30.255 --> 00:16:33.859
by making the cans
of Coke thinner.

00:16:33.859 --> 00:16:37.396 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we are becoming
more efficient.

00:16:37.396 --> 00:16:38.998
Also what we are doing,

00:16:38.998 --> 00:16:41.867
we are replacing some
resources with others.

00:16:41.867 --> 00:16:44.470
For example, we no
longer kill whales

00:16:46.972 --> 00:16:50.342
in order to scoop out spermaceti

00:16:50.342 --> 00:16:54.513 align:start size:88% position:13%
their brain mass, in
order to create candles.

00:16:54.513 --> 00:16:55.347
Today,

00:16:56.782 --> 00:16:59.818
we use fossil fuels
to power our lamps

00:16:59.818 --> 00:17:02.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
and our cars and our
cell phones and whatever.

00:17:02.154 --> 00:17:05.157
And maybe in the future 20,
40, or 50 years from now,

00:17:05.157 --> 00:17:07.459
we will have fusion reactors,

00:17:07.459 --> 00:17:10.629
which will basically
mean that electricity

00:17:10.629 --> 00:17:13.665
will become essentially free,

00:17:13.665 --> 00:17:15.467
will no longer have
to use fossil fuels

00:17:15.467 --> 00:17:18.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
and therefore, any worry
that people might have about

00:17:18.971 --> 00:17:22.608
us running out of oil or
natural gas will disappear.

00:17:22.608 --> 00:17:25.144 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. One of the interesting
things about this trend

00:17:25.144 --> 00:17:27.913
I thought was that your
answer basically said

00:17:27.913 --> 00:17:31.750
sort of trust that human
ideas can solve problems.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.053
And that in many
ways is so counter

00:17:34.053 --> 00:17:35.754
to what we hear coming
out of the left, now.

00:17:35.754 --> 00:17:38.424 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, we hear about
the green new deal

00:17:38.424 --> 00:17:41.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that this centralized
planning thing

00:17:41.527 --> 00:17:44.496
can somehow magically
just sort of solve

00:17:44.496 --> 00:17:47.466
all of these problems if we
just gave it enough power,

00:17:47.466 --> 00:17:50.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
but in many ways, what
power and money, of course.

00:17:50.602 --> 00:17:51.937
But in many ways
what you're saying

00:17:51.937 --> 00:17:53.839
is you decentralize things.

00:17:53.839 --> 00:17:56.341
You let people come
up with new ideas

00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:58.510
and suddenly we will
have a thinner can

00:17:58.510 --> 00:17:59.845 align:start size:81% position:19%
and less resources
will be taken,

00:17:59.845 --> 00:18:01.380
and it'll actually be cheaper

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.315
and there'll be more
readily available.

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.886 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The answer to climate
change is not going to come

00:18:07.886 --> 00:18:10.222
from central planning
and from restrictions

00:18:10.222 --> 00:18:13.292
for two reasons, let's divide
the world into two halves.

00:18:14.693 --> 00:18:17.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
In one half, you have
rich people like you and I

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:22.301 align:start size:97% position:3%
relatively rich, obviously,
living in democracies.

00:18:22.301 --> 00:18:26.105
Now we are prepared
to pay a little more

00:18:26.105 --> 00:18:27.439
for the protection
of the environment.

00:18:27.439 --> 00:18:29.475
We are willing to
put up with taxes,

00:18:29.475 --> 00:18:32.778 align:start size:97% position:3%
which are a little bit
higher, but not much higher.

00:18:32.778 --> 00:18:34.446
In fact, all the opinion polls

00:18:35.848 --> 00:18:37.649 align:start size:97% position:3%
and surveys suggest that
people are willing to put up

00:18:37.649 --> 00:18:41.386 align:start size:88% position:13%
a little more toward
cleaning of the climate.

00:18:41.386 --> 00:18:43.989 align:start size:88% position:13%
What they are not
prepared to do is to end

00:18:43.989 --> 00:18:48.994
the first world lifestyles as
they know it, for the benefit

00:18:51.497 --> 00:18:53.398
of environment or
climate change.

00:18:53.398 --> 00:18:56.034
- Right isn't that in essence,
what was happening in France,

00:18:56.034 --> 00:18:58.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they kept
raising taxes on gas

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:00.139
to do climate initiatives.

00:19:00.139 --> 00:19:02.274 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that basically
started riots.

00:19:03.675 --> 00:19:05.210
- And then you've got the
rise, the gorge homie.

00:19:07.679 --> 00:19:09.915
And so there's this one half

00:19:09.915 --> 00:19:13.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
rich people in
democracies will not vote

00:19:13.285 --> 00:19:16.421
to end civilization, full stop.

00:19:16.421 --> 00:19:18.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
Then you have the second
half of the world,

00:19:18.924 --> 00:19:21.793
let's say very poor people
or relatively poor people

00:19:21.793 --> 00:19:24.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
who live in countries,
which are not democracies,

00:19:25.964 --> 00:19:29.067 line:0%
places like that, places like
China and places like India,

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:32.538 line:0%
the governments there
are not going to forego

00:19:32.538 --> 00:19:36.708
the possibility of
rapid economic growth
and becoming rich,

00:19:36.708 --> 00:19:40.245 align:start size:97% position:3%
just because people in
rich countries demand that.

00:19:40.245 --> 00:19:43.582 align:start size:97% position:3%
We should demand it of
them because it is immoral.

00:19:43.582 --> 00:19:46.385
And those governments
are not going to do it

00:19:46.385 --> 00:19:47.986
because they're not suicidal.

00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:51.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
Even China, which is a
totalitarian, an awful regime,

00:19:51.456 --> 00:19:54.359
and which keeps saying we
are going to go carbon free

00:19:54.359 --> 00:19:55.794
by this date and other date,

00:19:55.794 --> 00:19:58.163
they're building a record number

00:19:58.163 --> 00:19:59.998
of coal powered

00:20:00.866 --> 00:20:02.935
power plants, as we speak.

00:20:02.935 --> 00:20:06.138 align:start size:84% position:16%
In other words,
they understand that

00:20:06.138 --> 00:20:10.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
there is nothing surer to
destroy the Communist Party's

00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:15.214
hold on power than denying the
Chinese people a better life.

00:20:15.814 --> 00:20:17.216
- Yeah.

00:20:17.216 --> 00:20:20.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that sort of an
offshoot of the rationale

00:20:20.252 --> 00:20:22.521
that got us out of
the Paris Accord.

00:20:22.521 --> 00:20:25.023 align:start position:0%
That in essence, these
countries were gonna keep doing

00:20:25.023 --> 00:20:27.292
these things because
they had to do them

00:20:27.292 --> 00:20:29.361
as they go through their
industrial revolution.

00:20:29.361 --> 00:20:31.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's talking say
about China and India,

00:20:31.063 --> 00:20:33.832
while we were going to put
regulations on ourselves,

00:20:33.832 --> 00:20:35.701
and pay for the brunt of it.

00:20:35.701 --> 00:20:38.437
And there was no enforcement
mechanism and everything else.

00:20:39.638 --> 00:20:41.807
- I think that was
a part of it is that

00:20:41.807 --> 00:20:43.342
the United States right, now.

00:20:43.342 --> 00:20:45.844
I don't believe I haven't
looked at those figures

00:20:45.844 --> 00:20:47.112
in a while,

00:20:47.112 --> 00:20:48.280
but I don't that
the United States

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:50.415 align:start size:84% position:16%
is the largest
polluter in the world.

00:20:50.415 --> 00:20:53.051 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that China and
India are above us.

00:20:53.051 --> 00:20:55.587
Now, here you have four
and a half billion people

00:20:56.488 --> 00:20:58.790
who are trying to get rich quick

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:01.326
and who are spewing a lot
of CO2 into the atmosphere

00:21:01.326 --> 00:21:03.395
and who are also very
competitive with us

00:21:03.395 --> 00:21:05.330
because they have
very low wages.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:10.102
So, to tie ourselves to
the Paris Climate Accord

00:21:10.102 --> 00:21:12.137
was the wrong way
to go about it.

00:21:12.137 --> 00:21:13.605
And you know what else happened

00:21:13.605 --> 00:21:18.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
also the United States has
decreased its emissions of CO2

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:22.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
per capita at a faster pace
than most places in the world.

00:21:22.214 --> 00:21:23.115
Why?

00:21:23.115 --> 00:21:23.915
Because

00:21:25.284 --> 00:21:28.186
we started getting rid
of our coal power plants

00:21:28.186 --> 00:21:31.056
and we started introducing
more and more natural gas

00:21:31.056 --> 00:21:33.225
into our electricity production.

00:21:33.225 --> 00:21:37.329
And natural gas burns or
rather emits much less CO2

00:21:37.329 --> 00:21:41.833
than coal, and so by
simply by innovating,

00:21:41.833 --> 00:21:44.069
by basically changing
the source of fuel

00:21:44.069 --> 00:21:45.637
from coal to natural gas,

00:21:45.637 --> 00:21:48.173 align:start size:88% position:13%
we were able to decrease
our CO2 emissions

00:21:48.173 --> 00:21:51.176
at a faster click than we
would have been obliged

00:21:51.176 --> 00:21:53.045
to do under Paris.

00:21:53.045 --> 00:21:55.113
- All right. So let's move
on to trend number four.

00:21:55.113 --> 00:21:57.215 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought this was interesting
'cause I see a lot of talk

00:21:57.215 --> 00:22:01.320
of this kind of thing online
related to peak population,

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:04.489
and that we're putting too
many people on this earth.

00:22:04.489 --> 00:22:05.457
Is it true?

00:22:07.092 --> 00:22:09.561
- It is not true.

00:22:09.561 --> 00:22:14.099
Right now, there are
7.8 billion of us.

00:22:14.099 --> 00:22:18.537
The global population will
peak in about 2060, 2070

00:22:18.537 --> 00:22:21.707
at roughly 9 and nine
and a a half billion.

00:22:21.707 --> 00:22:24.242
And then it will
start declining.

00:22:24.242 --> 00:22:25.677
There are a number of
studies done on this.

00:22:25.677 --> 00:22:27.746
The latest one came from Lancet,

00:22:27.746 --> 00:22:31.283 align:start position:0%
which is a very highly
regarded British medical paper.

00:22:31.283 --> 00:22:35.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what they have
concluded is that by 2100,

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:39.458 align:start size:97% position:3%
there will be anywhere between
6.8 and 8.8 billion people

00:22:39.458 --> 00:22:40.692
in the world.

00:22:40.692 --> 00:22:43.362 align:start size:88% position:13%
So roughly 1 billion,
less than we have today,

00:22:43.362 --> 00:22:45.030
or 1 billion more.

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:47.532
I'm very ambivalent
about this trend,

00:22:47.532 --> 00:22:52.204
because I think that because
humans are the only entities

00:22:52.204 --> 00:22:55.407
in the world capable of
actually producing ideas.

00:22:55.407 --> 00:22:57.843
If you are going to come
up with a cure for cancer,

00:22:57.843 --> 00:23:01.480
if you're going to come up for
a solution to climate change

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:04.282
and fusion reactor, you need
the human brain to do that.

00:23:04.282 --> 00:23:06.752
Artificial intelligence
is simply not there yet.

00:23:06.752 --> 00:23:08.920
So, fewer human beings we have,

00:23:10.122 --> 00:23:12.958
the harder it will be to
produce enough geniuses

00:23:12.958 --> 00:23:15.527
to come up with ideas,
to solve our problems.

00:23:15.527 --> 00:23:19.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
Nonetheless, the reality is
that the total fertility rate,

00:23:19.698 --> 00:23:24.603
which is to say the number of
babies that a woman can expect

00:23:24.603 --> 00:23:26.405
to have in her life,

00:23:26.405 --> 00:23:28.140
is now roughly around 2.4

00:23:29.574 --> 00:23:31.510
replacement level

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:32.744
is 2.1.

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:34.045 align:start size:84% position:16%
Replacement level
basically means that

00:23:34.045 --> 00:23:35.981
if you have fewer
babies than that,

00:23:35.981 --> 00:23:38.417 align:start size:84% position:16%
the global population
starts declining.

00:23:38.417 --> 00:23:41.920
In Europe, in places
like Czech Republic,

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:44.890
where my ancestors
are from or Slovakia,

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:49.661
the total fertility rate is
1.3, 1.4, rather than 2.1,

00:23:49.661 --> 00:23:52.631 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is needed just to
keep the population steady.

00:23:52.631 --> 00:23:56.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the only place
really, which is above

00:23:56.968 --> 00:23:58.437
2.1, 2.4

00:23:58.437 --> 00:23:59.671
is Africa.

00:23:59.671 --> 00:24:00.872
Population of Africa,

00:24:00.872 --> 00:24:02.574 align:start size:84% position:16%
is still going to
increase for a while,

00:24:02.574 --> 00:24:06.578
but globally we have
fewer and fewer babies

00:24:06.578 --> 00:24:09.481 align:start size:84% position:16%
being born per
woman. Why is that?

00:24:09.481 --> 00:24:11.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is because women are
getting education

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:13.585
and they are getting work.

00:24:13.585 --> 00:24:15.654
What happens when
a woman gets work,

00:24:15.654 --> 00:24:17.956
is that the opportunity
costs of staying at home

00:24:17.956 --> 00:24:20.292
and taking care of
children increases.

00:24:20.292 --> 00:24:22.594
She can do with her
life other things,

00:24:22.594 --> 00:24:25.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
she can earn a lot of
money in the marketplace.

00:24:25.964 --> 00:24:27.999 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so she has fewer
and fewer babies.

00:24:27.999 --> 00:24:32.237
This relationship, we
are seeing it no matter

00:24:32.237 --> 00:24:34.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
which continent
you're looking at it,

00:24:34.806 --> 00:24:36.074
we see it everywhere.

00:24:36.074 --> 00:24:39.978
And we expect that as
Africa becomes richer,

00:24:39.978 --> 00:24:43.415 align:start size:97% position:3%
African women will start
having fewer and fewer babies

00:24:43.415 --> 00:24:44.983
below the replacement level.

00:24:44.983 --> 00:24:48.253
- Yeah. Some of this stuff
feels like it must be hard

00:24:48.253 --> 00:24:49.721
to track because
if you were to say,

00:24:49.721 --> 00:24:52.791
Okay, let's look 100
years in the future

00:24:52.791 --> 00:24:57.128
with the trends of how people
are reproducing right now.

00:24:57.128 --> 00:24:59.531
Well, all sorts of
other trends change

00:24:59.531 --> 00:25:02.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
and food changes and
migration patterns change

00:25:02.501 --> 00:25:03.969
and everything else.

00:25:03.969 --> 00:25:05.537
So that, that explains
why it could be 1 billion

00:25:05.537 --> 00:25:06.738
over or under, right?

00:25:07.906 --> 00:25:11.743
- Correct. It was also
based on assumptions

00:25:11.743 --> 00:25:14.579 align:start size:88% position:13%
about economic growth
and access to education.

00:25:14.579 --> 00:25:17.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
We expect that the economy
will continue to grow.

00:25:17.082 --> 00:25:18.583 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, if the
economy stops growing

00:25:18.583 --> 00:25:22.420
or if there is a de-growth in
certain parts of the world,

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:24.689 align:start size:88% position:13%
then the relationship
between increased wealth

00:25:24.689 --> 00:25:26.558
and fewer babies obviously ends.

00:25:26.558 --> 00:25:28.293
- Yeah. When you guys are
tracking stuff like this,

00:25:28.293 --> 00:25:30.128 align:start size:88% position:13%
you've referenced,
COVID a couple of times,

00:25:30.128 --> 00:25:34.933
how do you plan for in the
models things like COVID.

00:25:34.933 --> 00:25:38.069 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, if we just took
this back eight months ago,

00:25:38.069 --> 00:25:41.339
nobody expected this sort
of a worldwide shutdown

00:25:41.339 --> 00:25:43.008
of an economy

00:25:43.008 --> 00:25:44.242
and the health stuff.

00:25:45.644 --> 00:25:47.913
- Right? I mean we don't
model anything in the book,

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:50.916
I do modeling on
natural resources.

00:25:50.916 --> 00:25:53.818
But the book itself is
a compilation of data

00:25:53.818 --> 00:25:55.554
from third-party sources,

00:25:55.554 --> 00:25:57.188 align:start size:88% position:13%
from inter-governmental
organizations,

00:25:57.188 --> 00:25:58.390
from the United Nations,

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:01.927 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the US Government
from Eurostat.

00:26:01.927 --> 00:26:04.763
We don't come up with any data.

00:26:04.763 --> 00:26:06.798 align:start size:84% position:16%
- By you guys I
meant the modelers.

00:26:06.798 --> 00:26:09.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
Not you guys specifically
writing the book.

00:26:10.402 --> 00:26:11.236
- Well,

00:26:12.737 --> 00:26:14.172
almost nobody saw this coming.

00:26:14.172 --> 00:26:16.274
I mean there were a few
people who were saying,

00:26:16.274 --> 00:26:17.576
this was a serious possibility,

00:26:17.576 --> 00:26:20.445
such as Bill Gates
and kudos to him

00:26:20.445 --> 00:26:21.780
for predicting five years ago,

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:24.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
that this was the
most serious problem

00:26:24.382 --> 00:26:27.586
that humanity was going to face
in the short to medium term.

00:26:27.586 --> 00:26:31.323 align:start size:97% position:3%
But most people were
worried about climate change

00:26:31.323 --> 00:26:34.092 align:start size:84% position:16%
and things which
could become serious.

00:26:34.092 --> 00:26:37.362
If we don't do anything,
if we sit on our hands,

00:26:37.362 --> 00:26:40.231
it could become serious
in 50, 60, 70 years.

00:26:40.231 --> 00:26:43.668 align:start size:84% position:16%
But we weren't looking
closer to home.

00:26:43.668 --> 00:26:46.571
We weren't looking
at our own bodies

00:26:46.571 --> 00:26:48.306
and inside of our own bodies.

00:26:48.306 --> 00:26:52.310 align:start size:84% position:16%
We weren't looking at
the natural evolution
that's going on

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.045
with viruses and bacteria,

00:26:54.045 --> 00:26:56.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
which are happening
at a much faster click

00:26:56.781 --> 00:26:59.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the body of every human
being and every animal.

00:26:59.517 --> 00:27:02.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think that it's
a rude awakening.

00:27:02.887 --> 00:27:06.191
It tells us something
about complacency.

00:27:06.191 --> 00:27:10.862
It tells us something about
appreciating modern life

00:27:13.098 --> 00:27:15.800
appreciating also human contact,

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.370
being deprived of
all of those things

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:20.438
I would hope would make us,

00:27:21.906 --> 00:27:23.975
more grateful

00:27:25.143 --> 00:27:27.679
for the world that
we had before COVID.

00:27:27.679 --> 00:27:29.848 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Speaking of grateful,
trend number five,

00:27:29.848 --> 00:27:31.182
the end of famine.

00:27:31.182 --> 00:27:33.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I remember
growing up in the '80s

00:27:33.385 --> 00:27:35.720 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what was
happening in Ethiopia

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:37.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that this was something
that you talked about,

00:27:37.922 --> 00:27:40.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
that there were places in
the world where people simply

00:27:40.925 --> 00:27:44.062
were starving to death.
This is being eradicated.

00:27:45.497 --> 00:27:47.732 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That's correct. I mean,
we are roughly the same age.

00:27:47.732 --> 00:27:50.502 align:start size:88% position:13%
And in the 1980s, I
remember very distinctly

00:27:50.502 --> 00:27:53.238
those horrific pictures
from the Horn of Africa,

00:27:53.238 --> 00:27:54.673
Ethiopia, Eritrea,

00:27:56.274 --> 00:27:58.443
children with swollen bellies

00:27:59.577 --> 00:28:02.247 align:start size:84% position:16%
from famine their
eyes covered by flies

00:28:02.247 --> 00:28:04.015
and that sort of thing.

00:28:04.015 --> 00:28:05.817
And we really thought
that this was going

00:28:05.817 --> 00:28:07.285
to be the future of humanity.

00:28:08.486 --> 00:28:10.021 align:start size:81% position:19%
A couple of things
have happened.

00:28:10.021 --> 00:28:13.024 align:start size:88% position:13%
One the world has become
a much more peaceful,

00:28:13.024 --> 00:28:15.226
which is to say that
the kinds of conflicts

00:28:15.226 --> 00:28:17.128
that we saw in Africa,

00:28:17.128 --> 00:28:19.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
partly as a result of
the cold war in the 1980s

00:28:22.333 --> 00:28:24.202
have pretty much disappeared.

00:28:24.202 --> 00:28:26.504
I mean, we still have civil
wars, but countries no longer

00:28:26.504 --> 00:28:29.441
really go into wars
against one another.

00:28:29.441 --> 00:28:33.078 align:start size:97% position:3%
The other thing that's
happened was that socialism

00:28:33.078 --> 00:28:34.713
and communism ended.

00:28:34.713 --> 00:28:37.782
And these countries have
opted for free markets

00:28:39.718 --> 00:28:41.152
and greater production

00:28:41.152 --> 00:28:44.355 align:start position:0%
or a better production, greater
production in agriculture.

00:28:44.355 --> 00:28:45.623
Once you don't have communism,

00:28:45.623 --> 00:28:46.991
once you're going
to have socialism,

00:28:46.991 --> 00:28:49.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
once farmers have a
stake in their own farms

00:28:49.928 --> 00:28:51.496
and in their own productivity,

00:28:51.496 --> 00:28:53.298
they tend to produce more food.

00:28:53.298 --> 00:28:55.900 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so globally, we have
seen tremendous gains

00:28:55.900 --> 00:28:59.104
in food productivity
per acre of earth.

00:29:00.538 --> 00:29:01.940
People no matter whether it's
they're in the United States

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:05.110
or in India, or China or Africa

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:08.446
are producing more food
as a result of which

00:29:10.348 --> 00:29:12.584
in Sub-Saharan Africa today,

00:29:12.584 --> 00:29:16.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
people have on average
access to as many calories

00:29:16.221 --> 00:29:18.690
as the Portuguese did
in the early 1960s.

00:29:20.125 --> 00:29:22.227
I just want to repeat that
today in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:29:22.227 --> 00:29:24.329
we no longer looking at famines.

00:29:24.329 --> 00:29:27.365
In fact, obesity is
becoming a problem,

00:29:27.365 --> 00:29:30.034
especially in urban areas.

00:29:30.034 --> 00:29:32.904
Instead, what we have
is food productivity

00:29:32.904 --> 00:29:35.573 align:start size:97% position:3%
and access to calories
equivalent to where Portugal

00:29:35.573 --> 00:29:37.108
was in the early 1960s.

00:29:37.108 --> 00:29:40.578 align:start size:97% position:3%
Nobody saw this coming. It's
a marvelous development.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:42.847
Today,

00:29:45.316 --> 00:29:46.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
the global production
of calories

00:29:46.551 --> 00:29:49.087
is about 3000 per
person per day.

00:29:49.087 --> 00:29:52.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
And United States, Department
of Agriculture recommends

00:29:52.257 --> 00:29:53.525
that people should be eating

00:29:53.525 --> 00:29:55.460
about 2000 calories
per person per day.

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:57.395
So, there's plenty
of food to go around.

00:29:57.395 --> 00:29:59.330 align:start size:97% position:3%
It doesn't mean that
everybody is well-nourished

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:02.000
because of course there are
always people in remote areas

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:04.569
or people be set by civil wars

00:30:04.569 --> 00:30:06.037
who don't have access to food.

00:30:06.037 --> 00:30:09.874 align:start size:97% position:3%
But we are already producing
one third more calories,

00:30:09.874 --> 00:30:12.610
than we hypothetically
need in the world today.

00:30:12.610 --> 00:30:14.546 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right? I mean,
that's more calories

00:30:14.546 --> 00:30:16.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
than I'm trying to
eat on an average day.

00:30:18.516 --> 00:30:21.252
- Which also means that
if the world's population

00:30:21.252 --> 00:30:25.023 align:start size:88% position:13%
by some miracle
increased from 8 billion

00:30:25.023 --> 00:30:28.226
to 11 or 12 billion,
which won't happen.

00:30:28.226 --> 00:30:30.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
But today we are
producing enough calories

00:30:30.795 --> 00:30:33.665
to feed the world
in 2100 and beyond.

00:30:33.665 --> 00:30:36.534 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, it's incredible.
So, trend number six.

00:30:36.534 --> 00:30:38.536 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought this was interesting
because I feel like

00:30:38.536 --> 00:30:40.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
no matter what
you're about to say,

00:30:40.271 --> 00:30:42.574 align:start size:84% position:16%
people just are not
going to believe this.

00:30:42.574 --> 00:30:45.543 align:start size:88% position:13%
A certain set of people
and you know what,

00:30:45.543 --> 00:30:47.579
we'll actually throw up
the image on this one,

00:30:47.579 --> 00:30:49.814
when we talk about
it for trend six.

00:30:49.814 --> 00:30:52.584 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is there's more
land for nature.

00:30:52.584 --> 00:30:55.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like people just
simply won't believe this,

00:30:55.119 --> 00:30:56.454
but take it away.

00:30:57.889 --> 00:30:59.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, what happens
as an increase

00:30:59.724 --> 00:31:02.493
of agricultural productivity

00:31:03.328 --> 00:31:04.229
first,

00:31:04.229 --> 00:31:05.063
and

00:31:06.164 --> 00:31:08.132
urbanization second

00:31:08.132 --> 00:31:10.668
is that there's more
land left for nature.

00:31:10.668 --> 00:31:11.502
So

00:31:12.871 --> 00:31:16.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
today, roughly 55% of
humanity lives in the cities

00:31:17.742 --> 00:31:20.378
by 2100 it will 80%.

00:31:20.378 --> 00:31:25.383 align:start position:0%
Sorry, by 2050, 80% of humanity
will be living in cities.

00:31:26.818 --> 00:31:31.155
What that means is that 80%
of people don't live on land.

00:31:32.657 --> 00:31:35.793
And what that means
is that all that land

00:31:35.793 --> 00:31:38.529
can now be returned to nature.

00:31:38.529 --> 00:31:41.833
Nature will re-concur it.
It doesn't need to be told.

00:31:41.833 --> 00:31:45.203
Animals will spread,
forests will grow.

00:31:45.203 --> 00:31:49.173
And of course, so that's
the result of urbanization.

00:31:49.173 --> 00:31:51.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
Also as a result of
agricultural productivity,

00:31:51.976 --> 00:31:55.179 align:start size:84% position:16%
we can produce
more food per acre.

00:31:55.179 --> 00:31:57.515
And that means that we
can feed more people

00:31:57.515 --> 00:32:02.420
on an average shrinking amount
of land as a result of which

00:32:03.421 --> 00:32:06.758
the land sparing as it is called

00:32:08.259 --> 00:32:09.494
is growing.

00:32:09.494 --> 00:32:12.730 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
I think the statistic
that we have in the book

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:15.800 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
is that in the last
20 years or so,

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:19.037 line:0%
we have saved the land the
size of Alaska for nature

00:32:19.037 --> 00:32:20.138
or something like that.

00:32:20.138 --> 00:32:21.673
- Yeah. It's just incredible.

00:32:21.673 --> 00:32:25.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean this is one of the ones
where something like COVID

00:32:25.243 --> 00:32:27.679
does seem like it changes
the calculation a bit,

00:32:27.679 --> 00:32:29.814
because right now at least,

00:32:29.814 --> 00:32:33.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're seeing people
move out of the cities

00:32:33.017 --> 00:32:34.986 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you think the overall
trends will be that

00:32:34.986 --> 00:32:36.654
eventually they will move back.

00:32:37.588 --> 00:32:39.090
- Well, see what happens.

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:41.225
I mean, after 911 people
were predicting the death

00:32:41.225 --> 00:32:43.828
of New York and New
York had of course,

00:32:43.828 --> 00:32:45.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
incredible two
decades ahead of it.

00:32:47.065 --> 00:32:51.903
You know, some people like
to live in like the idea

00:32:51.903 --> 00:32:54.305
of living off land and on land

00:32:54.305 --> 00:32:57.041 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the moment they start
farming and tending to horses

00:32:57.041 --> 00:32:58.509
and cows and et cetera,

00:32:58.509 --> 00:33:02.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
suddenly they start missing
the math or, you know,

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:04.015
the restaurants in the city

00:33:04.015 --> 00:33:06.284
and the company
of their friends.

00:33:06.284 --> 00:33:07.218
And so,

00:33:10.955 --> 00:33:15.360 align:start position:0%
I don't think that we are going
to see the death of a city.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:16.961
And

00:33:16.961 --> 00:33:21.065
moving forward I think that
urbanization will continue,

00:33:21.065 --> 00:33:23.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
especially in
developing countries

00:33:24.736 --> 00:33:27.505
where the need for nature
sparing is the greatest.

00:33:27.505 --> 00:33:29.607 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. Well, that's the
perfect segue to trend seven,

00:33:29.607 --> 00:33:31.876
which is planet city.

00:33:31.876 --> 00:33:32.977
Talk to me about that.

00:33:34.412 --> 00:33:38.316
- Well, planet city is good
for the environmental reasons

00:33:38.316 --> 00:33:39.851
that I outlined.

00:33:39.851 --> 00:33:43.121
One other reason that
I would want to...

00:33:43.121 --> 00:33:44.589
one other environmental reason,

00:33:44.589 --> 00:33:48.526
I would want to bring
up why cities are good

00:33:48.526 --> 00:33:52.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that on average,
a city dweller

